#{[17592186238339 :answer/value "-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186238465 :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186238765 :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186239827 :answer/value "-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186239848 :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}x"] [17592186244195 :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡y$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ only."] [17592186244216 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=x{y}^{2}.\n$$"] [17592186244216 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x,\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+x{y}^{-1}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+xz=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-x,\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}) & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse intervals where $1+Ce^{x^2/2}\\ne0$. The original equation also admits $y=0$; it is outside the requested nonzero family."] [17592186244217 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}y={x}^{2}{y}^{-4}.\n$$"] [17592186244217 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-3{x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=-4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{5}={x}^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}^{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z=5{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-15{x}^{2},\\,Q(x)=5{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-15{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (5{x}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}) & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{5}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}=\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}\\,⇒\\,y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the real fifth root and exclude points where $Ce^{5x^3}-1=0$. The original equation contains $y^{-4}$ and is undefined at $y=0$."] [17592186244218 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-x{y}^{3}.\n$$"] [17592186244218 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-x,\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{-2}=-x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z=2x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-2,\\,Q(x)=2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot (2x) \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z{e}^{-2x} & =2x{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x}) & =2x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-2x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}(2x+1)+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-2}=\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the displayed nonzero real family, require $Ce^{2x}-2x-1>0$ and retain both signs of the square root. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside this family."] [17592186244219 :question/problem "Find the general positive solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$ Choose constants and intervals with $Ce^{x/2}-1>0$."] [17592186244219 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z=1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z{e}^{-x/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-x/2} & =-{e}^{-x/2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =C{e}^{x/2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}=C{e}^{x/2}-1\\,⇒\\,y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z=\\sqrt y$, require $Ce^{x/2}-1>0$ for the requested positive family. Squaring a negative value of $z$ introduces a function that does not solve the original equation. The original equation also admits $y=0$, excluded by the positive condition."] [17592186244240 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWork on intervals with $x>0$."] [17592186244240 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{-1}=\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\sqrt{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $x>0$ and exclude zeros of $C-2x^{5/2}$. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside the requested nonzero family."] [17592186244246 :question/problem "Find the general positive solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4}.\n$$\n\nWork on intervals with $x>0$. Choose constants and intervals with $x^{9/4}+C>0$."] [17592186244246 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{1/4}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{4x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/4x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{1/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{1/4}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4}z & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4}) & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{1/4} & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{9/4}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=9{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/4}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/4}=\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}}\\,⇒\\,y={\\left(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}}\\right)}^{4}\\,.\n$$\n\nWork on $x>0$ and require $x^{9/4}+C>0$. This is necessary because $z=y^{1/4}>0$ in the requested positive family; taking the fourth power of a negative $z$ would not recover the same branch. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside this family."] [17592186244247 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186244247 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-1}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z=-{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & =-x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}\\,.\n$$\n\nUse an interval with $x\\ne0$ and $C-x\\ne0$. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside the requested nonzero family."] [17592186244248 :question/problem "Find the general positive solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$\n\nWork on intervals with $x>0$. Choose constants and intervals with $3\\sqrt{x}+C>0$."] [17592186244248 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nWork on $x>0$ and require $3\\sqrt x+C>0$, since $z=y^{3/2}>0$. Raising a negative base to $2/3$ does not invert the positive power $y^{3/2}$. The original equation is undefined at $y=0$."] [17592186247479 :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta +C"] [17592186247513 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +C"] [17592186247558 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{cos}x+C"] [17592186247587 :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{csc}x+C"] [17592186247619 :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186247682 :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sec}z+C"] [17592186247771 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +C"] [17592186247798 :answer/value "4\\operatorname{tan}z+C"]}
